# control: pass
# `expect_text_drawn … within=` is a BARRIER: the script does not advance past it
# until the text is DRAWN. The row click opens t6 as a preview and its reply
# renders some frames later. Without the barrier the very next line runs the
# next tick -- before the render -- and a plain `expect_no_text` of the reply
# passes VACUOUSLY (never drawn yet), telling nothing. With the barrier, the
# lines after it run only once the reply was on screen: the switch back to
# t2 then REMOVES a text that was provably there, and the `expect_no_text`
# that follows is a real absence, not a not-yet. The barrier reads rendered
# LABELS (whole), not the run-level registry a wrapped paragraph splits. The no-within control for
# ordinary behaviour is control_without_within_still_dies_at_thirty.
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["t2"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}
require_thread t2
wait_frames 12
# Baseline: no rendered label carries t6's reply before the click. The
# dump prints owners; a rendered one would be a line -- none is asserted by
# the registry read as well (the reply's fragment is in no run either).
expect_no_text "2.1M rows"
click_ui chat_row_id_t6
expect_text_drawn "61% through 2.1M rows" within=120
# Present as a rendered LABEL (the barrier's own read, re-asserted with a
# fresh budget) -- the registry may hold it only as wrapped runs.
expect_text_drawn "61% through 2.1M rows" within=30
click_ui tab_t2
expect_open t2
# t2's own transcript, by a fragment its bubble renders whole (the sidebar
# row title is ellipsised on screen, so it is not the target).
expect_text_drawn "Reconciled 4,812 accounts" within=60
# Real absence after the switch: the reply's label is no longer rendered --
# a registry read (run-level) that could only pass vacuously without the
# barrier before the switch, and is meaningful because the barrier proved
# the label was on screen first.
expect_no_text "2.1M rows"
